<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Giovanni Battista Tiepolo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1696">1696</a>–<a href="/years/1770">1770</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Tiepolo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Giovanni Battista Tiepolo was a Venetian painter, the leading European decorative painter of the 18th century. Known for luminous ceiling frescoes filled with figures in open skies, he worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a> from 1750 to 1753, where he painted the staircase and Imperial Hall of the Residence, and from 1762 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-of-spain">Charles III of Spain</a>, where he died.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giovanni-battista-tiepolo.jpg" alt="Giovanni Battista Tiepolo" width="320" height="423" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giovanni Battista Tiepolo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tiepolo,_Giovanni_Battista_-_Fresken_Treppenhaus_des_W%C3%BCrzburger_Residenzschlosses,_Szenen_zur_Apotheose_des_F%C3%BCrstbischofs,_Detail_Giovanni_Battista_Tiepolo_-_1750-1753.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
